  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM EDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nToday-tonight... Upper level high pressure will remain in place as \nan upper level trough across the eastern US pushes offshore. At the \nsurface, high pressure axis will continue to slowly shift south and \neastward, away from ECFL, as a surface boundary moves into the Deep \nSouth. Locally, light southwest to west winds will shift onshore in \nthe afternoon along the coast this afternoon with the formation of \nthe east coast sea breeze. The east coast sea breeze is forecast to \nform and slowly push inland, although a quicker progression inland \nis forecast across the Treasure Coast. The sea breeze collision is \nforecast to occur along the I-95 corridor late this afternoon/early \nevening. Storm motion will be light and northwesterly, which will \nbring activity back towards the coast and out into the Atlantic.  \n\nScattered showers and storms are forecast to form this afternoon \n(PoP 30-60%). Highest coverage will be across the north Brevard to \nOrange County northward. Isolated to scattered showers are currently \npushing across northern Osceola northward this afternoon from the \nwest as well as some showers forming along the sea breeze across the \nTreasure Coast. Forecast soundings show a moist profile, although \nnot as moist as it has been the past couple of days, with PW values \nof 1.8- 2.0\". The environment supports convection, with plenty of \ninstability (MUCAPE of 1900-2300 J/kg), sufficient buoyancy (lifted \nindex of -5 to -7), slightly cooler temperatures aloft (-7C at 500 \nmb), and sufficient downdraft potential (DCAPE 800-1000 J/kg) with \nsome drier air in the mid levels. Because of this, main storm \nhazards will be frequent lightning, gusty winds up to 50 mph,\nsmall hail, and torrential downpours capable of 2-4\" in a relatively\nshort period of time, promoting minor/nuisance flooding in urban \nand poor drainage areas. The Weather Prediction Center (WPC) \nmaintains a Marginal (level 1 of 4) Risk for excessive rainfall \nfor portions of east central Florida today, mainly from Vero Beach\nnorthward and as far inland as the Orlando Metro. \n\nHumid once again today with slightly above normal temperatures. \nAfternoon highs will be in the low to mid 90s with peak heat indices \nof 100-107F. Warm and muggy conditions will continue overnight, with \nlows in the low to mid 70s. \n\nMonday-Saturday... Upper level high pressure across the Great Plains \nwill shift eastward through mid week as the trough over the eastern \nUS shifts eastward and offshore through early next week. At the \nsurface, a weak frontal boundary will drop into the Deep south and \nnorthern Florida by early week. This frontal boundary will continue \nto shift southward to near north/central to central Florida into mid \nweek before washing out, but there remains some uncertainty with \nexactly how far the boundary will get before it washes out. The NBM \ncontinues to key in on deep moisture surging in proximity of the \nboundary, thus favoring higher than normal PoPs Monday and Tuesday \n(70-80%), Wednesday-Thursday (60-70%), with only a slight dip Friday-\nSaturday (50-60%). Storm motion will be fairly weak and erratic, \nhowever some activity will periodically push back towards the coast, \nespecially with stronger boundary collisions. Storm impacts will be \nsimilar to today, with locally heavy rainfall continuing to be a \nconcern. WPC has outlooked portions of east central Florida in a \nMarginal (level 1 of 4) Risk of Excessive Rainfall on Monday, and \nall of east central Florida on Tuesday. Seasonable temperatures and \nhumid conditions are forecast each day, with highs in the upper 80s \nto low 90s. Warm and muggy conditions will continue overnight, with \nlows in the low to mid 70s.",
